ref 17592186519285 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519507 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186519303 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519507 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519507 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519507 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186519285 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519507 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f41b096e-82c3-5dae-bf25-98903c7a8157" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519511 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186519004 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519511 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186519118 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519511 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186519122 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519511 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186519140 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519511 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186519167 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519511 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519511 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519511 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186519118 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519511 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "fe76e9c1-efee-513d-8f78-b81b7c1d3e0f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519513 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519424 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519513 #edb/ref 1069 "q-145100" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519513 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519513 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the following joint probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x+y}{4}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression of the conditional probability density function ${f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ for $0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519513 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0309d911-015b-5e4c-9346-30c2d14fb0b7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519513 #edb/ref 1074 "Using the formula for the marginal density function for $X,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{2x+y}{4}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(2x+y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {[2xy+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot [4x+2] \\\\  & =\\frac{2x+1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the full expression for ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x+1}{2}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow recall that the conditional probability density function of $Y$ given $X=x$ for two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability density function for $X$ and $Y,$ and\n\n- ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the marginal density function for $X.$\n\nNow, for $0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{2x+y}{4})}{(\\frac{2x+1}{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x+y}{4x+2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor other values of $x$ and $y,$ the function $f(x,y)$ equals $0.$ As a result, ${f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ will be equal to $0$ too.\n\nTherefore, the conditional probability density function is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x+y}{2(2x+1)}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519515 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519444 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519515 #edb/ref 1069 "q-145108" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519515 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519515 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the following conditional probability density function:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2y+x}{4y+2}, & \\,0<x<2,\\,0<y<1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(\\frac{1}{4}<X<Y\\,|\\,Y=\\frac{3}{4}).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519515 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "05787bc3-261f-5b82-bb66-810cc0eb28ae" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519515 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that \n\n$$\nP(X\\in A\\,|\\,Y=y)={\\int }_{A}{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ is the conditional probability density function of $X$ given $Y=y.$ \n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\frac{1}{4}<X<Y\\,|\\,Y=\\frac{3}{4}) & ={\\int }_{1/4}^{y}{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y=\\frac{3}{4})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/4}^{3/4}\\frac{(2\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}+x)}{(4\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}+2)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\int }_{1/4}^{3/4}(x+\\frac{3}{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{3x}{2}]}_{1/4}^{3/4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}[{x}^{2}+3x]_{1/4}^{3/4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}({(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}+3(\\frac{3}{4})-{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}-3(\\frac{1}{4})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}(\\frac{9}{16}+\\frac{9}{4}-\\frac{1}{16}-\\frac{3}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519525 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519420 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519525 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143637" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519525 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519525 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the following joint probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{e}^{x+y}}{2}, & \\,0\\le x\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡3,\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression of the conditional probability density function ${f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ for $0\\le x\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡3,\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519525 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1967c2eb-a9e1-5379-9ffd-3708e127b5c8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519525 #edb/ref 1074 "Using the formula for the marginal density function for $X,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\frac{{e}^{x+y}}{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}}{2}\\cdot {e}^{y}\\,{|}_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}}{2}\\cdot (2-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the full expression for ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{e}^{x}}{2}, & \\,0\\le x\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow recall that the conditional probability density function of $Y$ given $X=x$ for two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability density function for $X$ and $Y,$ and\n\n- ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the marginal density function for $X.$\n\nNow, for $0\\le x\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡3,\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡2,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{{e}^{x+y}}{2})}{(\\frac{{e}^{x}}{2})} \\\\  & ={e}^{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor other values of $x$ and $y,$ the function $f(x,y)$ equals $0.$ As a result, ${f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ will be equal to $0$ too.\n\nTherefore, the conditional probability density function is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{y}, & \\,0\\le x\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡3,\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519535 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519507 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519535 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143641" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519535 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519535 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the following joint probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5x{y}^{2}}{32} & \\,\\frac{y}{2}\\le x\\le \\sqrt{y},\\,0<y<4 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression of the conditional probability density function ${f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ for $\\frac{y}{2}\\le x\\le \\sqrt{y},\\,0<y<4.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519535 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3b02f20a-35d0-57b1-ada9-f1273f1f3904" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519535 #edb/ref 1074 "Using the formula for the marginal density function for $Y,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{y/2}^{\\sqrt{y}}\\frac{5x{y}^{2}}{32}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5{y}^{2}}{32}{\\int }_{y/2}^{\\sqrt{y}}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5{y}^{2}}{32}\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\,{|}_{y/2}^{\\sqrt{y}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5{y}^{2}}{64}\\cdot (y-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5{y}^{3}(4-y)}{256}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the full expression for ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5{y}^{3}(4-y)}{256}, & \\,0<y<4 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow recall that the conditional probability density function of $X$ given $Y=y$ for two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability density function for $X$ and $Y,$ and\n\n- ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is the marginal density function for $Y.$\n\nNow, for $\\frac{y}{2}\\le x\\le \\sqrt{y},\\,0<y<4,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{5x{y}^{2}}{32})}{(\\frac{5{y}^{3}(4-y)}{256})} \\\\  & =\\frac{8x}{y(4-y)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor other values of $x$ and $y,$ the function $f(x,y)$ equals $0.$ As a result, ${f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ will be equal to $0$ too.\n\nTherefore, the conditional probability density function is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8x}{y(4-y)}, & \\,\\frac{y}{2}\\le x\\le \\sqrt{y},\\,0<y<4, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519536 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519481 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519536 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143613" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519536 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519536 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{-3y}, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,y\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2x, & 0\\le x\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the continuous random variables $X$ and $Y,$ for which the conditional probability density function ${f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ and the marginal density function ${f}_{X}(x)$ are shown above. Find the expression of the joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ for $0\\le x\\le 1,\\,y\\ge 0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519536 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "403a78d0-86af-59ab-a2a0-082e022aaef5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519536 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that the conditional probability density function of $Y$ given $X=x$ for two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability density function for $X$ and $Y,$ and\n\n- ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the marginal density function for $X.$\n\nFrom the equation above, it follows that\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)\\cdot {f}_{X}(x).\n$$\n\nNow, for $0\\le x\\le 1,\\,y\\ge 0,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ={f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)\\cdot {f}_{X}(x) \\\\  & =3{e}^{-3y}\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =6x{e}^{-3y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor other values of $x$ and $y,$ the function ${f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ equals $0.$ As a result, the product will be equal to $0$ too.\n\nTherefore, the joint probability density function is the following:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}6x{e}^{-3y}, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,y\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519545 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519545 #edb/ref 1069 "q-145101" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519545 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519545 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the following joint probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+2y}{12}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression of the conditional probability density function ${f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ for $0\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le 1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519545 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "60bd267c-251c-5e47-b502-67f097fb7ef0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519545 #edb/ref 1074 "Using the formula for the marginal density function for $Y,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{{x}^{2}+2y}{12}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{12}+\\frac{y}{6})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{36}+\\frac{xy}{6}]}_{x=0}^{x=3} \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{36}+\\frac{3y}{6}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{y}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2y+3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the full expression for ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2y+3}{4}, & \\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow recall that the conditional probability density function of $X$ given $Y=y$ for two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability density function for $X$ and $Y,$ and\n\n- ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is the marginal density function for $Y.$\n\nNow, for $0\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le 1,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{{x}^{2}+2y}{12})}{(\\frac{2y+3}{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+2y}{3(2y+3)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor other values of $x$ and $y,$ the function $f(x,y)$ equals $0.$ As a result, ${f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ will be equal to $0$ too.\n\nTherefore, the conditional probability density function is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+2y}{3(2y+3)}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519548 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519467 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519548 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143607" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519548 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519548 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{2}, & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}, & 0\\le x\\le 2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the continuous random variables $X$ and $Y,$ for which the joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ and the marginal density function ${f}_{X}(x)$ are shown above. Find the expression of the conditional probability density function ${f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ for $0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519548 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6a8bad50-df40-5364-9a82-59b2638eab96" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519548 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that the conditional probability density function of $Y$ given $X=x$ for two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability density function for $X$ and $Y,$ and\n\n- ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the marginal density function for $X.$\n\nNow, for $0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{2})}{(\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =3{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor other values of $x$ and $y,$ the function $f(x,y)$ equals $0.$ As a result, ${f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ will be equal to $0$ too.\n\nTherefore, the conditional probability density function is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}3{y}^{2}, & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519563 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519456 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519563 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143619" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519563 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519563 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the following joint probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}3x, & \\,y<x<1\\,,\\,0<y<1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFind $P(\\frac{1}{4}<X\\,|\\,Y=\\frac{1}{6}).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519563 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "91a2a3a1-8ae1-5b13-976f-9bc558602e26" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519563 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that \n\n$$\nP(X\\in A\\,|\\,Y=y)={\\int }_{A}{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)\\,\\text{d}y,\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ is the conditional probability density function of $X$ given $Y=y.$  \n\nUsing the formula for the marginal density function for $Y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{y}^{1}3x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}]}_{y}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}(1-{y}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the full expression for ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}(1-{y}^{2}), & \\,0<y<1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow recall that the conditional probability density function of $X$ given $Y=y$ for two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)}.\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability density function for $X$ and $Y,$ and\n\n- ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is the marginal density function for $Y.$\n\nNow, for $y<x<1\\,,\\,0<y<1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3x}{(\\frac{3}{2}(1-{y}^{2}))} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{1-{y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor other values of $x$ and $y,$ the function $f(x,y)$ equals $0.$ As a result, ${f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ will be equal to $0$ too.\n\nTherefore, the conditional probability density function is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x}{1-{y}^{2}}, & \\,0<y<x<1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute our probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\frac{1}{4}<X\\,|\\,Y=\\frac{1}{6}) & =P(\\frac{1}{4}<X\\,|\\,Y=\\frac{1}{6}) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/4}^{1}{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y=\\frac{1}{6})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/4}^{1}\\frac{2x}{(1-{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{2})}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/4}^{1}\\frac{2x}{(\\frac{35}{36})}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{35}{\\int }_{1/4}^{1}2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{35}{[{x}^{2}]}_{1/4}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{35}(1-\\frac{1}{16}) \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{28}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519568 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519504 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519568 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143620" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519568 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519568 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the following conditional probability density function:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2x, & \\,0\\le x\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(\\frac{1}{5}<X\\,|\\,Y=\\frac{4}{3}).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519568 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a05be7fd-94f3-5604-ad03-bc6e74737579" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519568 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that \n\n$$\nP(X\\in A\\,|\\,Y=y)={\\int }_{A}{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ is the conditional probability density function of $X$ given $Y=y.$ \n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\frac{1}{5}<X\\,|\\,Y=\\frac{4}{3}) & ={\\int }_{1/5}^{1}{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y=\\frac{4}{3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/5}^{1}2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[{x}^{2}]_{1/5}^{1} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519578 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519441 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519578 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143610" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519578 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519578 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}5y\\sqrt{x}, & 0<x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt{x} \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5x\\sqrt{x}}{2}, & 0<x\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the continuous random variables $X$ and $Y,$ for which the joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ and the marginal density function ${f}_{X}(x)$ are shown above. Find the expression of the conditional probability density function ${f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ for $0<x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt{x}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519578 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b3c1aae3-e457-548d-bfd0-11222eb5ea0e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519578 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that the conditional probability density function of $Y$ given $X=x$ for two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability density function for $X$ and $Y,$ and\n\n- ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the marginal density function for $X.$\n\nNow, for $0<x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt{x},$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{5y\\sqrt{x}}{(\\frac{5x\\sqrt{x}}{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2y}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor other values of $x$ and $y,$ the function $f(x,y)$ equals $0.$ As a result, ${f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ will be equal to $0$ too.\n\nTherefore, the conditional probability density function is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2y}{x}, & 0<x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt{x} \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519584 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519502 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519584 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143622" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519584 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519584 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the following joint probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}y\\sqrt{x}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 1,0\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFind $P(\\frac{1}{4}<Y<X\\,|\\,X=\\frac{3}{4}).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519584 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d2c3b01f-8b1d-5c9b-95df-4bf4aec9fac1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519584 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that \n\n$$\nP(Y\\in A\\,|\\,X=x)={\\int }_{A}{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)\\,\\text{d}y,\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ is the conditional probability density function of $Y$ given $X=x.$ \n\nUsing the formula for the marginal density function for $X,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{3}{4}y\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\sqrt{x}{\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\sqrt{x}{[\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{x}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the full expression for ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{x}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow recall that the conditional probability density function of $Y$ given $X=x$ for two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)}.\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability density function for $X$ and $Y,$ and\n\n- ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the marginal density function for $X.$\n\nNow, for $0<x<1,0<y<2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{4}y\\sqrt{x})}{(\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{x})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor other values of $x$ and $y,$ the function $f(x,y)$ equals $0.$ As a result, ${f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ will be equal to $0$ too.\n\nTherefore, the conditional probability density function is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}y, & \\,0\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute our probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\frac{1}{4}<Y<X\\,|\\,X=\\frac{3}{4}) & =P(\\frac{1}{4}<Y<\\frac{3}{4}\\,|\\,X=\\frac{3}{4}) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/4}^{3/4}{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x=\\frac{3}{4})\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/4}^{3/4}\\frac{1}{2}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}]}_{1/4}^{3/4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{9}{16}-\\frac{1}{16}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519592 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519511 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519592 #edb/ref 1069 "q-144941" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519592 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519592 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{10}, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 3 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{x}^{2}+9}{10}, & 0\\le x\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the continuous random variables $X$ and $Y,$ for which the joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ and the marginal density function ${f}_{X}(x)$ are shown above. Find the expression of the conditional probability density function ${f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ for $0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519592 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e6d9b032-eda8-55d5-b69c-37b94f5fdf98" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519592 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that the conditional probability density function of $Y$ given $X=x$ for two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability density function for $X$ and $Y,$ and\n\n- ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the marginal density function for $X.$\n\nNow, for $0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 3,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{10})}{(\\frac{3{x}^{2}+9}{10})} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{3{x}^{2}+9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor other values of $x$ and $y,$ the function $f(x,y)$ equals $0.$ As a result, ${f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ will be equal to $0$ too.\n\nTherefore, the conditional probability density function is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{3{x}^{2}+9}, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 3 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519605 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519451 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519605 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143633" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519605 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519605 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the following joint probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3x{y}^{2}}{14}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,-1\\le y\\le 3 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression of the conditional probability density function ${f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ for $0\\le x\\le 1,\\,-1\\le y\\le 3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519605 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f873ff79-08e6-53a2-8403-85dc4a081919" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519605 #edb/ref 1074 "Using the formula for the marginal density function for $X,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{3}\\frac{3x{y}^{2}}{14}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3x}{14}{\\int }_{-1}^{3}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3x}{14}\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}\\,{|}_{-1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{14}\\cdot [27-(-1)] \\\\  & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the full expression for ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2x, & \\,0\\le x\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow recall that the conditional probability density function of $Y$ given $X=x$ for two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability density function for $X$ and $Y,$ and\n\n- ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the marginal density function for $X.$\n\nNow, for $0\\le x\\le 1,\\,-1\\le y\\le 3,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3x{y}^{2}}{14})}{2x} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{28}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor other values of $x$ and $y,$ the function $f(x,y)$ equals $0.$ As a result, ${f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ will be equal to $0$ too.\n\nTherefore, the conditional probability density function is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{28}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,-1\\le y\\le 3 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519607 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519460 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519607 #edb/ref 1069 "q-144943" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519607 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519607 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{x}^{2}+3y}{8}, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3y+1}{8}, & 0\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the continuous random variables $X$ and $Y,$ for which the joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ and the marginal density function ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are shown above. Find the expression of the conditional probability density function ${f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ for $0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519607 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fde66fe1-1f42-5587-ae07-4dc1d6de7d02" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519607 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that the conditional probability density function of $X$ given $Y=y$ for two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability density function for $X$ and $Y,$ and\n\n- ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is the marginal density function for $Y.$\n\nNow, for $0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3{x}^{2}+3y}{8})}{(\\frac{3y+1}{8})} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}+3y}{3y+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor other values of $x$ and $y,$ the function $f(x,y)$ equals $0.$ As a result, ${f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ will be equal to $0$ too.\n\nTherefore, the conditional probability density function is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{x}^{2}+3y}{3y+1}, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
